1
00:00:56,681 --> 00:00:58,015
Hi, boss.

2
00:00:58,182 --> 00:01:00,351
Driver sustained heavy lacerations
and head trauma

3
00:01:00,476 --> 00:01:02,436
but believe it or not
they're saying he'll live.

4
00:01:02,562 --> 00:01:03,854
Lucky.

5
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:06,857
Victim's Sharon Warwick, 23.

6
00:01:07,024 --> 00:01:09,193
She went off of a balcony,
room 914.

7
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:11,404
Rigsby's up there right now
with the forensics team.

8
00:01:11,529 --> 00:01:13,531
Now off the bruise on the cheek
and the ripped vest

9
00:01:13,656 --> 00:01:15,032
we're thinking it's murder.

10
00:01:15,616 --> 00:01:18,202
- Catering staff?
- Actually, she was a cast member.

11
00:01:18,327 --> 00:01:19,370
Show's called "Torch".

12
00:01:19,495 --> 00:01:21,664
It's a story about intersecting
lives and loves

13
00:01:21,789 --> 00:01:23,833
in a New York night club
in the 1950s.

14
00:01:23,958 --> 00:01:25,960
The event tonight
was a fund-raiser for the show.

15
00:01:26,168 --> 00:01:28,879
Multiple witnesses say
the time of death is 8:01 p.m.

16
00:01:29,005 --> 00:01:30,798
right when the performance
was set to begin.

17
00:01:30,923 --> 00:01:32,758
- Well, what was she doing up there?
- We don't know yet.

18
00:01:32,883 --> 00:01:34,635
She was I.D.'d
by Warren Dodge, the producer.

19
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:37,013
- He's in there with the director.
- All right.

20
00:01:41,684 --> 00:01:43,060
Poor Sharon.

21
00:01:43,227 --> 00:01:45,563
Tragedy. Absolute tragedy.

22
00:01:46,564 --> 00:01:47,607
But you know what?

23
00:01:47,732 --> 00:01:49,609
We're gonna regroup and we'll move
forward from this, Bran

24
00:01:49,734 --> 00:01:51,068
better and stronger.
You'll see.

25
00:01:51,193 --> 00:01:53,696
Warren, our star is in a cab blotto
our second lead is dead.

26
00:01:53,821 --> 00:01:55,740
How do you propose
we do better and stronger?

27
00:01:55,865 --> 00:01:56,907
- Bran, listen...
- Mr. Dodge?

28
00:01:57,033 --> 00:01:58,159
- Yes?
- I'm Agent Lisbon.

29
00:01:58,284 --> 00:01:59,660
I need to ask you
some questions.

30
00:01:59,785 --> 00:02:01,871
Of course. Of course.
Can you give me just one minute?

31
00:02:01,996 --> 00:02:04,582
Bran, we are going
to get "Torch" to Broadway.

32
00:02:04,707 --> 00:02:07,668
We are, all right?
I'll take care of the investors.

33
00:02:07,793 --> 00:02:09,503
Hanover is solid,
that's 50% of our money.

34
00:02:09,629 --> 00:02:11,839
It's only the other investors
we have to worry about, all right?

35
00:02:11,964 --> 00:02:14,425
Your job is to keep calm and reassure
the cast and crew, all right?

36
00:02:14,550 --> 00:02:15,635
- Mr. Dodge.
- Yes?

37
00:02:15,801 --> 00:02:17,762
- And you are?
- Bran McTavish, the director.

38
00:02:17,887 --> 00:02:20,306
Did either of you actually
see Sharon Warwick fall?

39
00:02:20,431 --> 00:02:23,434
- I was inside. The show was starting.
- And I arrived right after it happened.

40
00:02:23,559 --> 00:02:25,936
Do you have any idea what
she was doing up in room 914?

41
00:02:26,062 --> 00:02:28,522
Well, we were using that area
as a dressing room for this event.

42
00:02:28,648 --> 00:02:30,274
She was supposed
to be down in the ballroom.

43
00:02:30,399 --> 00:02:31,734
She was third cue.

44
00:02:31,859 --> 00:02:32,902
- Third cue?
- Yeah.

45
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:36,155
We had, actors scattered about
dressed as hotel guests, workers, caterers.

46
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:37,948
It was Bran's idea
to start the show.

47
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:41,118
Yeah, a guest breaks out into song
then the bellboy, then the waitress.

48
00:02:41,243 --> 00:02:42,411
That was Sharon.

49
00:02:42,536 --> 00:02:44,038
She was supposed to be
in the ballroom.

50
00:02:44,163 --> 00:02:45,206
You've both been really helpful.

51
00:02:45,331 --> 00:02:48,751
If you wouldn't mind going up to the room
while we start the investigation

52
00:02:48,876 --> 00:02:50,586
- I'd be grateful.
- Anything we can do.

53
00:02:59,887 --> 00:03:00,930
Hey, Lisbon.

54
00:03:01,055 --> 00:03:02,264
"Where the hell are you?"

55
00:03:02,598 --> 00:03:04,642
"Don't tell me
you're at the hospital."

56
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:07,186
- I'm not at the hospital.
- "We caught a case."

57
00:03:07,311 --> 00:03:08,646
"You're needed
at the crime scene."

58
00:03:08,771 --> 00:03:10,106
I'll be there soon.

59
00:03:10,231 --> 00:03:11,982
You're at the hospital,
aren't you?

60
00:03:12,108 --> 00:03:13,776
You told me to tell you
I'm not at the hospital.

61
00:03:13,901 --> 00:03:15,569
What do you want?
Make up your mind.

62
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:16,946
Get out of there.

63
00:03:17,071 --> 00:03:20,116
If Jason Lennon wakes up
and if he talks

64
00:03:20,241 --> 00:03:22,451
"and if he knows
Red John's identity"

65
00:03:22,576 --> 00:03:26,455
"Agent Kirkland and Homeland Security
are perfectly capable of handling it."

66
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:28,290
I disagree.

67
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:29,792
You're playing with fire.

68
00:03:29,917 --> 00:03:33,254
There are worse things to play with.
Oh, gotta go.

69
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:38,092
- Agent Kirkland.
- How did you get in?

70
00:03:38,217 --> 00:03:42,304
- This wing is closed to visitors.
- My Aunt Daphne's in room 10.

71
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:45,725
Kidney failure.
Poor dear. Drinks like a fish.

72
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:48,602
You must be broken up about it.
Look at you. You're a mess.

73
00:03:50,813 --> 00:03:52,606
We both know why you're here.

74
00:03:53,232 --> 00:03:56,736
Although I find your need to talk to Lennon
now interesting.

75
00:03:56,861 --> 00:03:59,739
You did stand by while
Lorelei Martins shot him.

76
00:03:59,864 --> 00:04:01,449
How's he doing?

77
00:04:01,574 --> 00:04:03,325
He'll live to talk to us.

78
00:04:03,826 --> 00:04:06,579
And I'll let you know if he has
anything interesting to say.

79
00:04:08,289 --> 00:04:09,915
You're always good
to talk to you, Patrick.

80
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:13,252
But I think you should go now
before we start disturbing the patients.

81
00:04:14,336 --> 00:04:16,547
- This way, sir.
- Yeah. I'm going. That's my arm.

82
00:04:16,672 --> 00:04:18,632
You have two of your own.
Thank you.

83
00:04:19,425 --> 00:04:21,260
How did you get in here?

84
00:04:21,427 --> 00:04:22,845
Who let you in?

85
00:04:23,179 --> 00:04:24,847
Well, if I told you that,
I wouldn't be able to get in

86
00:04:24,972 --> 00:04:26,682
when I come back, would I?

87
00:04:42,529 --> 00:04:45,115
did anybody have access
to the suite besides Sharon?

88
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:48,076
This served as a sort of communal
dressing area for this event

89
00:04:48,202 --> 00:04:49,912
so, everybody, really.

90
00:04:50,037 --> 00:04:52,873
The whole cast plus myself,
Dodge, and, Sylvia.

91
00:04:52,998 --> 00:04:54,541
Hey, have you spoken to Hanover?

92
00:04:54,666 --> 00:04:56,668
I wanna make sure he hears
about this from us first.

93
00:04:56,794 --> 00:04:58,504
Sylvia, you worry
about the other investors.

94
00:04:58,629 --> 00:04:59,713
Let me deal with Hanover, all right?

95
00:04:59,838 --> 00:05:01,340
This is Sylvia Clare, our publicist.

96
00:05:01,465 --> 00:05:04,051
Sylvia, Agent Lisbon
is with the CBI.

97
00:05:04,426 --> 00:05:07,971
- Who's Hanover?
- Our patron. Our main investor.

98
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:09,723
Oh, Jane, there you are.

99
00:05:09,848 --> 00:05:13,435
{\an8}- This is the...
- No need. I just caught up in the elevator.

100
00:05:14,561 --> 00:05:19,358
Dodge, McTavish, and Clare
producer, director, publicist.

101
00:05:19,525 --> 00:05:22,986
{\an8}Was Sharon having any trouble
with the cast or crew?

102
00:05:23,111 --> 00:05:24,279
No, no. Not at all.

103
00:05:24,404 --> 00:05:26,323
We're a very happy family
here at "Torch."

104
00:05:26,448 --> 00:05:27,533
{\an8}Yes, of course.

105
00:05:27,658 --> 00:05:30,661
{\an8}That's exactly what a good publicist
should say, isn't it?

106
00:05:31,578 --> 00:05:34,289
Where were you when
the victim fell, Ms. Clare?

107
00:05:35,165 --> 00:05:36,625
Oh, I was in the ballroom.

108
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:40,504
{\an8}Mr. Dodge, what made you wanna cast
Sharon Warwick as the second lead?

109
00:05:40,629 --> 00:05:43,173
According to this, she doesn't
have much theater experience.

110
00:05:43,549 --> 00:05:45,133
{\an8}Her paragraph's very short.

111
00:05:45,259 --> 00:05:47,803
{\an8}Well, Sharon came out here
from New York

112
00:05:47,928 --> 00:05:50,222
{\an8}and she just knocked Bran out
with her audition.

113
00:05:50,347 --> 00:05:52,140
{\an8}And, I trust his judgment.

114
00:05:52,266 --> 00:05:55,727
{\an8}She's a natural dancer, big voice
quick learner, and she was hungry.

115
00:05:55,853 --> 00:05:56,895
{\an8}That's all you need.

116
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:58,355
Well, what was in it for her?

117
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:00,649
I mean, it's a revival in the sticks.

118
00:06:00,774 --> 00:06:03,944
{\an8}She was already
a well-known singer in New York.

119
00:06:04,361 --> 00:06:06,822
{\an8}She wanted to be a part
of something special.

120
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:09,324
{\an8}Exactly. I mean, we're starting
out here in Sacramento

121
00:06:09,449 --> 00:06:11,618
{\an8}but "Torch" will be on Broadway
in six months.

122
00:06:11,743 --> 00:06:13,245
{\an8}And a Tony contender in nine.

123
00:06:14,538 --> 00:06:17,040
{\an8}Did the cast have much contact
with the locals?

124
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,543
{\an8}I don't know. I don't...
I don't think so.

125
00:06:19,668 --> 00:06:21,044
{\an8}Oh, I wouldn't think
they'd have the time.

126
00:06:21,169 --> 00:06:23,171
{\an8}They would have been right
into rehearsals straightaway.

127
00:06:23,297 --> 00:06:25,299
- Yeah. That's right.
- So, yeah.

128
00:06:25,465 --> 00:06:26,842
{\an8}So you think like me.

129
00:06:27,009 --> 00:06:30,178
{\an8}The killer is connected to your musical.
Any guesses?

130
00:06:32,639 --> 00:06:34,391
{\an8}No? Okay. Thanks.

131
00:06:36,935 --> 00:06:38,770
{\an8}I'm sorry. Who is that?

132
00:06:38,937 --> 00:06:40,314
{\an8}He's a consultant.

133
00:06:41,481 --> 00:06:42,733
{\an8}Thank you for your time.

134
00:06:47,487 --> 00:06:50,532
{\an8}The suspect pool is huge.
It's gonna be a nightmare.

135
00:06:50,741 --> 00:06:53,827
{\an8}Why start on such a pessimistic foot?
Why are you so glum?

136
00:06:53,994 --> 00:06:55,621
{\an8}I call you up, and you're
creeping around Lennon

137
00:06:55,746 --> 00:06:58,540
{\an8}getting into Kirkland's business.
Of course I'm glum.

138
00:06:58,665 --> 00:07:00,834
{\an8}- Oh, that.
- Yeah. That.

139
00:07:00,959 --> 00:07:03,629
{\an8}We look bad enough after
the whole Lorelei Martins mess.

140
00:07:03,795 --> 00:07:07,049
{\an8}Lennon is a direct link to Red John.
I need to talk to him.

141
00:07:07,174 --> 00:07:10,886
{\an8}You need to try and start following
the rules for a while.

142
00:07:11,261 --> 00:07:13,972
{\an8}And if you must break the rules
break them on your own time.

143
00:07:14,097 --> 00:07:16,433
{\an8}When am I not on my own time?

144
00:07:20,646 --> 00:07:25,192
{\an8}"Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh
Better than nothing"

145
00:07:25,359 --> 00:07:27,361
"oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh"

146
00:07:27,486 --> 00:07:30,072
"You're better than nothing,
Than nothing"

147
00:07:30,197 --> 00:07:32,574
- "Whoa, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh"
- Grace.

148
00:07:33,158 --> 00:07:34,534
It's so good to see you.

149
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:36,036
- Hi.
- How was L.A.?

150
00:07:36,161 --> 00:07:39,706
I learned a lot of really great stuff but
I don't know how anybody lives there.

151
00:07:39,831 --> 00:07:40,874
I'm really happy to be home.

152
00:07:41,041 --> 00:07:43,669
- Well, welcome back to the grindstone.
- Yeah. What's with the music?

153
00:07:43,794 --> 00:07:46,713
Our victim's a singer.
She was pushed out of a window.

154
00:07:46,838 --> 00:07:49,007
It's kind of catchy, huh?
It was an Internet hit.

155
00:07:49,132 --> 00:07:51,051
She comes out here to star
in a musical and gets killed.

156
00:07:51,176 --> 00:07:53,011
Van Pelt! Welcome back.

157
00:07:53,178 --> 00:07:54,805
Thanks, boss.
It's good to be back.

158
00:07:54,930 --> 00:07:56,223
It's good to see you.

159
00:07:56,431 --> 00:07:57,516
How we doing so far?

160
00:07:57,641 --> 00:07:59,810
Good. Found out that Warwick
was actually a local.

161
00:07:59,935 --> 00:08:03,063
Really? The people from the theater company
seemed to think she was from New York.

162
00:08:03,188 --> 00:08:04,815
No, I spoke to a Polly Datch.

163
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:06,858
Said she was Warwick's foster mother
here in Sacramento.

164
00:08:06,984 --> 00:08:08,110
She's on her way to CBI.

165
00:08:08,235 --> 00:08:10,112
What about Warwick's timeline
the night of the murder?

166
00:08:10,237 --> 00:08:12,114
I've spoken to about
half the cast so far.

167
00:08:12,239 --> 00:08:14,908
Bad news is they were leaving the door
to that room unlocked for ease of access.

168
00:08:15,033 --> 00:08:16,952
So anyone could have been in there
when she died?

169
00:08:17,077 --> 00:08:19,246
Yeah. The good news is we got
a whole lot of video

170
00:08:19,371 --> 00:08:20,956
and photos to work off.

171
00:08:21,081 --> 00:08:24,251
So the cast left the room at around
a quarter of 8:00 to get into position.

172
00:08:24,459 --> 00:08:25,669
Warwick was with them.

173
00:08:26,211 --> 00:08:28,338
She leaves the ballroom at 7:52.

174
00:08:28,839 --> 00:08:31,216
At 8:01, she hits the limo.

175
00:08:31,550 --> 00:08:33,969
Was anyone else from production missing
during that period?

176
00:08:34,094 --> 00:08:35,512
I can't tell just yet.
I'll keep digging.

177
00:08:35,637 --> 00:08:37,264
Did anybody leave
in a hurry after she fell?

178
00:08:37,389 --> 00:08:39,975
Yeah, one.
Deandra Sunderland, the star.

179
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:42,436
Now the rumor among the cast
is that she's got a drinking problem

180
00:08:42,561 --> 00:08:45,647
so they think she's probably getting sauced
in one of the, hotel bars.

181
00:08:45,772 --> 00:08:47,816
A fading diva with a drinking problem?
That's unusual.

182
00:08:47,941 --> 00:08:50,861
Yeah. Check this out.
This is her leaving out the back door.

183
00:08:51,903 --> 00:08:54,531
- Well, when was that?
- About half-hour after the murder.

184
00:08:54,656 --> 00:08:56,867
What about the surveillance footage
from the hotel?

185
00:08:56,992 --> 00:08:59,536
Cameras in the lobby and all the elevators.
It's being sent over.

186
00:08:59,661 --> 00:09:01,788
What about the investor... Hanover?
Do you have anything on him?

187
00:09:01,913 --> 00:09:03,749
Yeah, the cast had a lot to say
about him, too.

188
00:09:03,874 --> 00:09:05,375
Apparently he can buy Delaware.

189
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:08,295
We also found his arrival in the party
footage the publicist sent over.

190
00:09:09,504 --> 00:09:10,589
I looked him up on the Internet.

191
00:09:10,714 --> 00:09:13,133
He's a currency trader,
and he likes the arts.

192
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:16,094
Hey, boss, Polly Datch,
victim's foster mom...

193
00:09:16,219 --> 00:09:17,763
- wants to talk to you.
- All right.

194
00:09:30,484 --> 00:09:33,487
Mrs. Datch?
I'm Agent Lisbon.

195
00:09:34,738 --> 00:09:37,699
You were Sharon Warwick's
foster parent?

196
00:09:37,824 --> 00:09:40,077
- Yes, ma'am.
- For how long?

197
00:09:40,786 --> 00:09:43,371
Sharon came to live with me
when she was 12.

198
00:09:43,538 --> 00:09:45,999
Never knew her biological parents.

199
00:09:46,124 --> 00:09:49,586
I stay close with most of my kids
but Sharon was special.

200
00:09:52,089 --> 00:09:54,049
That's the man you need to find...

201
00:09:54,257 --> 00:09:57,094
- Billy Racine.
- Who is he?

202
00:09:57,511 --> 00:09:59,429
No-good thug, that's who.

203
00:09:59,554 --> 00:10:02,390
- Is he an ex-boyfriend?
- He was her one bad choice.

204
00:10:03,725 --> 00:10:06,812
Had she been in contact with Racine
since you came back to town?

205
00:10:07,187 --> 00:10:08,730
I'm sure of it.

206
00:10:08,855 --> 00:10:10,982
And I think he assaulted her
a week ago.

207
00:10:11,441 --> 00:10:12,818
You think?

208
00:10:12,943 --> 00:10:15,612
I don't really know because
she wouldn't talk about it.

209
00:10:15,946 --> 00:10:18,740
She brushed it off,
called it a botched mugging.

210
00:10:18,949 --> 00:10:22,077
But I'm certain it was him.
Why else wouldn't she report it?

211
00:10:22,369 --> 00:10:24,579
Had he been violent with her
in the past?

212
00:10:24,704 --> 00:10:26,414
Sharon said no, but...

213
00:10:27,124 --> 00:10:29,668
I know Billy Racine
is a violent man.

214
00:10:36,508 --> 00:10:37,551
Sure.

215
00:10:37,676 --> 00:10:40,095
Of course, Mr. Hanover.
Thank you for your time.

216
00:10:40,470 --> 00:10:41,930
Finally found Hanover?

217
00:10:42,055 --> 00:10:43,515
Yeah. He didn't see
the crash either

218
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:44,891
but he said he'd be
happy to talk to us

219
00:10:45,016 --> 00:10:47,394
- when he gets back in town.
- Back in town?

220
00:10:47,519 --> 00:10:49,980
He had to fly to an art opening.
A piece he just had to have.

221
00:10:50,188 --> 00:10:51,857
- Rich people.
- What'd you get?

222
00:10:51,982 --> 00:10:53,650
Lisbon got this
from the foster mom.

223
00:10:53,775 --> 00:10:56,027
Name's Billy Racine.
Ex-boyfriend.

224
00:10:57,237 --> 00:10:58,864
Foster mother thinks
he could have done it.

225
00:10:58,989 --> 00:11:00,031
Guy's had a life.

226
00:11:00,198 --> 00:11:01,825
Multiple assaults, extortion.

227
00:11:02,409 --> 00:11:04,161
That name sounds familiar.

228
00:11:07,289 --> 00:11:08,540
"Racine William."

229
00:11:08,665 --> 00:11:10,458
He's on the list of people
who purchased tickets

230
00:11:10,584 --> 00:11:11,835
to the show's fund-raiser.

231
00:11:13,253 --> 00:11:14,838
What was he doing there?

232
00:11:14,963 --> 00:11:16,715
I'll see if I can
track him down.

233
00:11:24,431 --> 00:11:25,473
Thanks.

234
00:11:26,266 --> 00:11:28,268
What were you doing
at the fund-raiser for "Torch"

235
00:11:28,435 --> 00:11:31,479
- two nights ago?
- Supporting the arts.

236
00:11:32,397 --> 00:11:34,566
Did you have any contact with
Sharon Warwick that evening?

237
00:11:35,817 --> 00:11:37,027
If I did, what's it matter?

238
00:11:37,152 --> 00:11:38,737
You two had quite the tempestuous
relationship

239
00:11:38,862 --> 00:11:40,488
before she moved to New York.

240
00:11:40,822 --> 00:11:43,783
Shouting matches.
You even put your fist through a wall.

241
00:11:44,284 --> 00:11:46,578
Did you pick up where you left off
when she came back a month ago?

242
00:11:46,703 --> 00:11:48,788
Don't even try to pin this on me.

243
00:11:48,955 --> 00:11:50,749
I know what's happened,
and I didn't kill her.

244
00:11:50,874 --> 00:11:51,917
Why should I believe you?

245
00:11:52,042 --> 00:11:54,502
Because I would never
put my hands on that girl.

246
00:11:54,628 --> 00:11:55,670
I loved her.

247
00:11:55,795 --> 00:11:57,172
I think you loved her
maybe too much.

248
00:11:58,590 --> 00:12:00,634
She rejected you
you didn't take "no" for an answer

249
00:12:00,759 --> 00:12:02,469
you stalked her to the fund-raiser.

250
00:12:02,719 --> 00:12:06,056
And when she rejected you again
your hands got the better of you.

251
00:12:08,308 --> 00:12:10,810
- I went there to protect her.
- Protect her from what?

252
00:12:10,936 --> 00:12:12,437
She got mugged last week.

253
00:12:12,729 --> 00:12:13,772
Said she fought 'em off

254
00:12:13,897 --> 00:12:15,232
and scratched one of them up
pretty good

255
00:12:15,357 --> 00:12:16,483
but I could see
she was shook.

256
00:12:16,608 --> 00:12:18,276
What does that have to do
with the fund-raiser?

257
00:12:18,735 --> 00:12:21,363
Sharon thought someone
from the show set it up.

258
00:12:22,030 --> 00:12:24,074
So when I heard
they were doing that party...

259
00:12:25,075 --> 00:12:26,660
You went to find her attackers?

260
00:12:27,244 --> 00:12:29,079
I was gonna tune some people up.

261
00:12:30,205 --> 00:12:33,708
But Sharon said "be easy."
Said she'd handle it.

262
00:12:33,833 --> 00:12:36,711
- Did she say who she suspected?
- She wouldn't say.

263
00:12:37,462 --> 00:12:39,881
She was scared I was gonna do
something bad to 'em.

264
00:12:40,006 --> 00:12:41,049
And she was right.

265
00:12:41,174 --> 00:12:43,093
So you're telling me everything
was good between you two?

266
00:12:45,136 --> 00:12:47,180
You've been talking to
that old foster lady, huh?

267
00:12:47,973 --> 00:12:49,641
She never liked me.

268
00:12:50,058 --> 00:12:51,977
You can't believe
what she says.

269
00:13:05,448 --> 00:13:08,243
"Why do fools fall in love?"

270
00:13:10,078 --> 00:13:13,206
Okay. One second. One second.
that's your cue, Deandra.

271
00:13:16,918 --> 00:13:19,838
Tell me again...why am I helping
you audition replacements?

272
00:13:20,338 --> 00:13:23,925
Well, because our investor performance
was rescheduled

273
00:13:24,050 --> 00:13:25,218
and, we have to be ready.

274
00:13:25,343 --> 00:13:28,805
So if we could try it one more time
that would be wonderful.

275
00:13:29,639 --> 00:13:33,768
I don't think you really need me
for this, Bran, okay?

276
00:13:34,102 --> 00:13:35,478
I'll be in my dressing room.

277
00:13:39,024 --> 00:13:40,150
No problem!

278
00:13:41,276 --> 00:13:42,485
Okay...

279
00:13:42,861 --> 00:13:45,655
Mandy, Bridget, Howard
from where we left off.

280
00:13:45,780 --> 00:13:47,449
One, two, three.

281
00:13:48,199 --> 00:13:53,538
"Love is a losing game
Love can be a shame"

282
00:13:53,663 --> 00:13:56,082
"I know of a fool you see"

283
00:13:56,207 --> 00:13:58,543
"For that fool is me"

284
00:13:58,668 --> 00:14:01,546
"Tell me why"

285
00:14:01,671 --> 00:14:04,924
Sorry. Sorry. Wait.
This is a closed rehearsal.

286
00:14:05,050 --> 00:14:06,551
Yeah. We're investigating a murder.

287
00:14:06,676 --> 00:14:09,346
Well, you won't find any clues here.

288
00:14:09,679 --> 00:14:13,183
Actually, we could learn a lot
from watching a rehearsal.

289
00:14:13,767 --> 00:14:14,809
Oh, really?

290
00:14:14,934 --> 00:14:18,313
Yes. The way a person sings
says a great deal about them.

291
00:14:18,521 --> 00:14:21,441
For instance, Bridget,
you have a very sweet tone.

292
00:14:21,775 --> 00:14:22,817
Thank you.

293
00:14:22,942 --> 00:14:26,488
Yes, with nice shades
of pent-up anger and envy.

294
00:14:26,613 --> 00:14:29,949
You hated Sharon on principle
yet you may come to replace her.

295
00:14:30,075 --> 00:14:34,079
So obviously, you had very good
motive to want to kill her.

296
00:14:34,204 --> 00:14:36,915
But I don't think you have
the stomach or the strength

297
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:38,750
to push someone off a balcony.

298
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,004
You, Mandy, on the other hand
are perfectly capable of murder.

299
00:14:43,129 --> 00:14:45,632
You have a very strong instrument.
You could play Sharon's part.

300
00:14:45,757 --> 00:14:50,178
But are you hungry enough to kill?
Do you have that kind of passion?

301
00:14:50,595 --> 00:14:52,597
I don't know. Hmm?

302
00:14:53,139 --> 00:14:55,308
Oh, don't even look at me.
Thank you.

303
00:14:55,433 --> 00:14:57,602
Oh, please. Pitchy.

304
00:14:57,769 --> 00:14:59,729
Very... pitchy

305
00:15:01,898 --> 00:15:03,400
You done?

306
00:15:03,608 --> 00:15:06,611
Yes. Well, that leaves you,
Mr. McTavish.

307
00:15:06,820 --> 00:15:10,323
Vain. Controlling.
Neurotic. Egotistical.

308
00:15:10,448 --> 00:15:11,741
Sleeping with...

309
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:15,120
at least two of your actors.

310
00:15:17,914 --> 00:15:19,332
Are you a killer?

311
00:15:20,166 --> 00:15:22,127
You know, I never did
catch your name.

312
00:15:24,671 --> 00:15:25,713
Damn.

313
00:15:25,839 --> 00:15:26,881
I was sure it was you.

314
00:15:27,006 --> 00:15:28,299
Never mind. Name's Jane.

315
00:15:28,425 --> 00:15:30,260
Nice to meet you all.
Break a leg.

316
00:15:40,019 --> 00:15:43,648
Well, I thought
you sounded... great.

317
00:15:54,991 --> 00:15:56,659
- He's upsetting the performers.
- I know. I know.

318
00:15:56,784 --> 00:15:58,411
- He's gonna throw their performances off.
- Bran, I'll take care of it.

319
00:15:58,536 --> 00:15:59,871
- All right.
- All right?

320
00:16:07,503 --> 00:16:08,629
Mr. Jane.

321
00:16:09,464 --> 00:16:12,049
Please. Your presence
isn't good for morale.

322
00:16:12,175 --> 00:16:13,885
Everyone seems
to be handling it okay.

323
00:16:14,051 --> 00:16:16,637
You've asked your questions.
Is it necessary that you remain?

324
00:16:16,762 --> 00:16:19,140
We'll stay out of your way.
You'll hardly know we're here.

325
00:16:19,599 --> 00:16:21,642
Pardon me. Okay,
so I've gotten confirmations

326
00:16:21,767 --> 00:16:22,894
from all of our bigger investors.

327
00:16:23,019 --> 00:16:24,437
They will all be
at the rescheduled preview.

328
00:16:24,562 --> 00:16:26,063
Excellent. And, Hanover?

329
00:16:26,189 --> 00:16:28,274
- Still need to double confirm.
- Good. Good.

330
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:30,735
- Busy, busy.
- Indeed.

331
00:16:33,779 --> 00:16:35,281
If you'll excuse me,
I have to take this.

332
00:16:35,406 --> 00:16:36,741
Sure.

333
00:16:51,005 --> 00:16:52,048
Excuse me.

334
00:16:52,965 --> 00:16:54,133
Sorry to bother you.

335
00:16:54,467 --> 00:16:57,887
I was wondering if you knew
what jazz hands were?

336
00:16:58,095 --> 00:17:01,224
Sure. It's the use of big hand movements
in choreography.

337
00:17:01,807 --> 00:17:02,850
Why do you ask?

338
00:17:02,975 --> 00:17:05,645
Oh, I just overheard the director
ranting about jazz hands

339
00:17:05,770 --> 00:17:08,231
so I was curious to know
what got him so worked up.

340
00:17:08,731 --> 00:17:10,024
Ranting how?

341
00:17:10,149 --> 00:17:12,777
Well apparently, he hate,
hate, hates jazz hands.

342
00:17:12,902 --> 00:17:16,113
And if he sees one jazzy little hand
that dancer is out of the show.

343
00:17:17,114 --> 00:17:19,617
Oh. Good to know.
Thanks.

344
00:17:19,742 --> 00:17:21,494
No worries. you might
wanna pass that on

345
00:17:21,619 --> 00:17:23,746
to the girl that comes up
after you.

346
00:17:23,871 --> 00:17:25,373
Break a leg.

347
00:17:31,045 --> 00:17:32,588
Okay, Maggie, you're up.

348
00:17:32,838 --> 00:17:35,341
So what exactly are we looking for
with these auditions?

349
00:17:35,675 --> 00:17:37,301
We'll know when we see it.

350
00:17:38,052 --> 00:17:40,972
- You like musicals?
- Oh, what's not to like?

351
00:18:16,591 --> 00:18:20,678
Wow. They're so good.
How many more are there?

352
00:18:20,803 --> 00:18:22,054
I'm not sure.

353
00:18:22,221 --> 00:18:25,308
A troublemaker should reveal
herself very shortly.

354
00:18:56,297 --> 00:18:58,883
No! No! No, no, no,
no, no, no, no.

355
00:18:59,008 --> 00:19:00,051
What are you doing, Bridget?

356
00:19:00,176 --> 00:19:02,178
Suddenly we were in
a cabaret bar in Idaho.

357
00:19:02,303 --> 00:19:04,263
I thought you wanted
bigger hand movement.

358
00:19:04,388 --> 00:19:05,431
What gave you that idea?

359
00:19:05,556 --> 00:19:09,435
I'm sorry, but this,
might indirectly be my doing.

360
00:19:11,103 --> 00:19:12,813
I told the first dancer
that the director

361
00:19:12,938 --> 00:19:17,151
would fire anyone
that added big hand movements.

362
00:19:18,277 --> 00:19:19,320
What?

363
00:19:19,445 --> 00:19:21,822
And she, along with everyone
else who auditioned

364
00:19:22,198 --> 00:19:24,158
made sure that the dancer
that came after them

365
00:19:24,283 --> 00:19:25,993
didn't use the jazz hands

366
00:19:26,118 --> 00:19:27,495
so that they didn't
lose their job...

367
00:19:27,620 --> 00:19:28,954
until Bridget.

368
00:19:29,622 --> 00:19:31,332
Mandy came before me.

369
00:19:31,791 --> 00:19:33,250
You can't be serious.

370
00:19:33,376 --> 00:19:36,587
Only a scheming
and conniving person

371
00:19:36,754 --> 00:19:40,299
would so callously try
to get a fellow cast mate fired.

372
00:19:40,424 --> 00:19:43,552
There's no telling how far
someone like you will go.

373
00:19:44,178 --> 00:19:46,138
Is he telling the truth, Mandy?

374
00:19:46,263 --> 00:19:47,640
What does it matter?

375
00:19:47,807 --> 00:19:49,016
At least the hands distract away

376
00:19:49,141 --> 00:19:51,477
from the dreadful
syncopation problem you have.

377
00:19:52,228 --> 00:19:54,230
- You wanted me to fail?
- That's not hard.

378
00:19:54,355 --> 00:19:55,606
You bitch.

379
00:19:56,816 --> 00:19:58,067
- Rigsby?
- Okay.

380
00:19:58,192 --> 00:20:00,027
I'm sorry. What did you just call me?

381
00:20:00,194 --> 00:20:01,862
- Oh, I'm sorry...
- Get your hands off me!

382
00:20:01,987 --> 00:20:03,739
You mind stepping in
and handling this?

383
00:20:05,866 --> 00:20:07,618
Ladies, ladies, ladies...

384
00:20:07,743 --> 00:20:09,120
I have an errand to run.

385
00:20:09,328 --> 00:20:12,581
Hey. Hey. Hey.

386
00:20:13,165 --> 00:20:14,750
Okay, let's...

387
00:20:18,045 --> 00:20:19,296
Help!

388
00:20:20,756 --> 00:20:21,882
Okay.

389
00:20:22,258 --> 00:20:24,969
I know you're only talking to me
to get information out of me

390
00:20:25,094 --> 00:20:26,971
but it's been nice.

391
00:20:27,763 --> 00:20:29,223
You make me smile.

392
00:20:29,807 --> 00:20:31,851
Well, what's worth
more than a smile?

393
00:20:32,935 --> 00:20:33,978
True enough.

394
00:20:38,065 --> 00:20:40,276
Mr. Lennon is pretty much stable.

395
00:20:41,110 --> 00:20:42,153
His blood volume is up

396
00:20:42,278 --> 00:20:43,904
and the doctors could bring him
out of the coma

397
00:20:44,029 --> 00:20:45,489
as early as tomorrow.

398
00:20:45,614 --> 00:20:47,324
Thank you. That's very good to know.

399
00:20:48,367 --> 00:20:49,910
What is all this about?

400
00:20:50,911 --> 00:20:53,456
And why are the federal guys
so weird and creepy?

401
00:20:53,748 --> 00:20:56,083
They are? How are they
weird and creepy?

402
00:20:56,667 --> 00:20:58,335
Like they're not
what they seem.

403
00:20:58,794 --> 00:21:01,464
Like one day they might
pull their faces off

404
00:21:01,589 --> 00:21:03,841
and there'd be alien lizards
underneath, you know?

405
00:21:03,966 --> 00:21:06,969
I know. Not impossible,
but very unlikely.

406
00:21:08,179 --> 00:21:11,766
- I know. They just have that vibe.
- I understand.

407
00:21:13,601 --> 00:21:16,270
Will you call me when they bring
Mr. Lennon out of his coma?

408
00:21:16,395 --> 00:21:20,149
- Sure.
- Thank you. I appreciate that.

409
00:21:24,195 --> 00:21:25,988
Interesting set of scratch marks.

410
00:21:26,989 --> 00:21:28,073
What happened?

411
00:21:29,450 --> 00:21:30,493
Cat.

412
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:35,164
Sharon Warwick fought back
against one of her attackers

413
00:21:35,289 --> 00:21:37,583
when she was mugged.
Know anything about that?

414
00:21:40,252 --> 00:21:44,673
You know they found D.N.A. samples
under Sharon's nails after the murder?

415
00:21:45,341 --> 00:21:48,260
It's amazing how a little D.N.A.
can tie two people together.

416
00:21:48,761 --> 00:21:51,597
Okay. The mugging, that was me.

417
00:21:51,722 --> 00:21:54,058
I just wanted to scare her a little bit.
I didn't kill her.

418
00:21:54,266 --> 00:21:55,434
Who was your accomplice?

419
00:21:57,353 --> 00:21:58,646
A guy named Joey.

420
00:21:58,771 --> 00:22:00,314
He's my partner
in salsa class.

421
00:22:01,190 --> 00:22:02,399
And why did you want
to scare her?

422
00:22:02,525 --> 00:22:05,528
Because she was just some girl
with an okay voice and no resume

423
00:22:05,653 --> 00:22:07,696
and there she was headlining
with Deandra.

424
00:22:07,822 --> 00:22:08,989
And that made you mad?

425
00:22:09,114 --> 00:22:10,991
Second lead should have
been me, not Sharon.

426
00:22:11,116 --> 00:22:12,451
So when she showed up
for the fund-raiser

427
00:22:12,576 --> 00:22:14,537
ready to launch
a star-making turn

428
00:22:14,703 --> 00:22:16,497
you snapped and you pushed her
off that balcony.

429
00:22:16,622 --> 00:22:18,332
I told you I didn't kill her.

430
00:22:18,457 --> 00:22:20,501
Well, you've already admitted
to organizing an assault.

431
00:22:20,626 --> 00:22:21,710
Why is it so crazy to think

432
00:22:21,836 --> 00:22:23,295
that you didn't decide
to finish the job?

433
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:24,880
Because I didn't
want her dead.

434
00:22:25,005 --> 00:22:26,757
I just wanted her to leave.

435
00:22:30,344 --> 00:22:31,512
If you're looking
for suspects

436
00:22:31,637 --> 00:22:33,889
you might wanna check out
the star of the show.

437
00:22:34,056 --> 00:22:35,391
Deandra Sunderland?

438
00:22:35,766 --> 00:22:37,977
The queen of mean.

439
00:22:38,269 --> 00:22:40,604
What makes you think Sunderland
could have killed Warwick?

440
00:22:41,146 --> 00:22:45,025
Because the director wanted to
replace Deandra with Sharon.

441
00:22:45,317 --> 00:22:46,777
Everybody knew it.

442
00:22:46,902 --> 00:22:50,030
I mean she's too old to be
playing the ingenue, right?

443
00:22:50,155 --> 00:22:52,116
Did you see Sunderland
the night of the murder?

444
00:22:53,033 --> 00:22:57,413
No. She was nowhere to be found
when the overture began.

445
00:22:57,538 --> 00:22:59,081
What about after the accident?

446
00:23:00,124 --> 00:23:02,293
Not until rehearsal the next morning.

447
00:23:14,889 --> 00:23:17,975
"Every time"

448
00:23:18,601 --> 00:23:23,480
"We say goodbye'

449
00:23:24,857 --> 00:23:27,610
"I die"

450
00:23:28,569 --> 00:23:30,237
"A little"

451
00:23:31,697 --> 00:23:34,867
"Every time"

452
00:23:35,159 --> 00:23:40,080
"We say goodbye"

453
00:23:40,873 --> 00:23:45,544
"I wonder why"

454
00:23:45,794 --> 00:23:47,755
"A little"

455
00:23:49,006 --> 00:23:51,717
Yes, Mr. Hanover. Excellent.

456
00:23:52,509 --> 00:23:55,012
Yeah. Yes.
No, that sounds good.

457
00:23:56,722 --> 00:23:59,433
Okay. Sounds good. Sure.

458
00:23:59,558 --> 00:24:01,435
We'll see you very soon.
Thanks.

459
00:24:02,061 --> 00:24:04,229
Sorry. How long
you gonna keep Mandy?

460
00:24:04,355 --> 00:24:07,608
A while. That's what we do
when people break the law.

461
00:24:07,733 --> 00:24:10,235
Okay, look, agent, I'm a pro.
I can spin this tragedy.

462
00:24:10,402 --> 00:24:12,780
It can even add an air of fascination
to the production.

463
00:24:13,030 --> 00:24:14,531
But if you arrest the cast

464
00:24:14,657 --> 00:24:17,076
right before a performance
for the investors

465
00:24:17,201 --> 00:24:18,827
that makes my job
rather difficult.

466
00:24:18,953 --> 00:24:22,873
You see, I need my investors
to stay... invested.

467
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:24,083
Well, brace yourself

468
00:24:24,208 --> 00:24:26,794
because now I need to talk
to Deandra Sunderland.

469
00:24:29,546 --> 00:24:30,798
This is a nightmare.

470
00:24:32,549 --> 00:24:38,222
"There's no love song finer'"

471
00:24:39,390 --> 00:24:42,017
"But how strange"

472
00:24:42,309 --> 00:24:44,019
"The change"

473
00:24:45,062 --> 00:24:52,111
"From major to minor"

474
00:24:53,237 --> 00:24:54,530
Miss Sunderland.

475
00:24:55,572 --> 00:24:58,867
My apologies for interrupting.
We need to talk.

476
00:24:59,451 --> 00:25:02,037
I'm rehearsing here.
I asked not to be disturbed.

477
00:25:02,621 --> 00:25:04,248
This is important.

478
00:25:04,456 --> 00:25:07,334
Where were you when Sharon Warwick
fell off the balcony?

479
00:25:10,087 --> 00:25:11,505
I was in the ballroom.

480
00:25:11,839 --> 00:25:14,341
All of our evidence
points to the contrary.

481
00:25:14,883 --> 00:25:18,095
Everybody involved in the production
seems to think you were off drinking.

482
00:25:19,221 --> 00:25:20,973
I think you might have been
with Sharon.

483
00:25:22,725 --> 00:25:24,893
I think you found out that
McTavish was planning

484
00:25:25,019 --> 00:25:27,312
on replacing you with her,
and you got mad.

485
00:25:28,564 --> 00:25:31,233
He wanted Sharon
to replace me?

486
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:33,819
She was studying film
of all of your performances

487
00:25:33,944 --> 00:25:35,863
and memorizing your lines.

488
00:25:39,700 --> 00:25:41,118
You don't understand.

489
00:25:42,619 --> 00:25:44,621
She would if you told her the truth.

490
00:25:50,377 --> 00:25:52,046
You sing beautifully.

491
00:25:54,131 --> 00:25:57,134
- How long have you been out there?
- Long enough.

492
00:25:59,053 --> 00:26:02,681
You know, I kept wondering
what brought Sharon Warwick

493
00:26:02,806 --> 00:26:03,891
back to Sacramento.

494
00:26:04,016 --> 00:26:05,893
She left Sacramento
because she wanted something

495
00:26:06,018 --> 00:26:07,603
and now she's back.

496
00:26:08,771 --> 00:26:10,564
It's obviously connected
to "Torch"

497
00:26:11,607 --> 00:26:13,067
but it couldn't be acclaim
she was looking for

498
00:26:13,192 --> 00:26:15,194
'cause she got that
in New York City.

499
00:26:17,738 --> 00:26:19,990
And then I heard you sing,
and it came to me.

500
00:26:23,410 --> 00:26:26,080
What is it that every foster child
is looking for?

501
00:26:26,705 --> 00:26:29,458
Lisbon, I'd like you to meet
Sharon Warwick's mother.

502
00:26:48,266 --> 00:26:49,434
I was...

503
00:26:50,811 --> 00:26:53,230
Very young when I had Sharon.

504
00:26:55,816 --> 00:27:00,320
Her father was my director,
and he was married.

505
00:27:03,532 --> 00:27:07,035
The hardest thing I ever did in my life
was giving her away.

506
00:27:08,286 --> 00:27:10,455
When did she learn
she was your daughter?

507
00:27:11,414 --> 00:27:15,418
She came to my dressing room
after the first day of rehearsals.

508
00:27:15,585 --> 00:27:17,212
Must have been a shock.

509
00:27:17,921 --> 00:27:21,216
I felt so happy seeing the person
that she had become.

510
00:27:22,759 --> 00:27:24,511
So talented, so...

511
00:27:25,971 --> 00:27:27,430
full of life.

512
00:27:28,431 --> 00:27:30,267
Why did you keep it a secret?

513
00:27:33,728 --> 00:27:35,981
Guilt. Shame.

514
00:27:37,274 --> 00:27:38,859
Something else.

515
00:27:40,110 --> 00:27:41,862
You're hiding
something else.

516
00:27:44,656 --> 00:27:46,825
You're quite a clever policeman,
aren't you?

517
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:52,205
I have multiple sclerosis.

518
00:27:54,166 --> 00:27:56,334
Diagnosed six months ago.

519
00:27:57,961 --> 00:27:59,254
I'm sorry.

520
00:28:01,756 --> 00:28:03,925
Symptoms of the disease
is what made people think

521
00:28:04,050 --> 00:28:05,677
you were drinking.

522
00:28:05,802 --> 00:28:08,471
Well, I've been sober
for 14 years.

523
00:28:09,514 --> 00:28:11,099
Haven't touched a drop.

524
00:28:12,934 --> 00:28:16,104
I heard the whispers
behind my back.

525
00:28:17,272 --> 00:28:19,399
I thought it might be better to quit.

526
00:28:20,734 --> 00:28:22,277
Sharon was so mad.

527
00:28:24,070 --> 00:28:26,114
Already protective towards me.

528
00:28:27,407 --> 00:28:29,075
She told me to be strong.

529
00:28:30,076 --> 00:28:31,244
And you had an attack

530
00:28:31,369 --> 00:28:33,747
right before the first performance,
didn't you?

531
00:28:33,914 --> 00:28:35,665
You didn't have your medication.

532
00:28:36,791 --> 00:28:37,834
Yes.

533
00:28:37,959 --> 00:28:40,837
Sharon said she'd go up
to the suite and get it for you.

534
00:28:44,257 --> 00:28:46,384
She was an incredible young woman.

535
00:28:47,844 --> 00:28:50,597
That's why I can't quit
the show.

536
00:28:50,805 --> 00:28:52,557
I have to go on with it...

537
00:28:53,391 --> 00:28:55,101
to honor my child.

538
00:29:01,149 --> 00:29:02,192
Morning.

539
00:29:02,317 --> 00:29:03,610
- Morning.
- Morning.

540
00:29:03,818 --> 00:29:06,321
We've been through all the photos, videos
and security footage.

541
00:29:06,488 --> 00:29:08,448
Cross-indexed every guest, hotel worker

542
00:29:08,573 --> 00:29:10,659
attendee, and person
connected to the play.

543
00:29:10,784 --> 00:29:12,786
All their movements are
accounted for, except for him.

544
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:15,664
Berndt Hanover, the investor?

545
00:29:15,830 --> 00:29:19,376
Yeah, here he is before the murder
getting into the elevator.

546
00:29:20,085 --> 00:29:22,087
He goes to the floor
Sharon Warwick was killed on

547
00:29:22,212 --> 00:29:24,798
and gets off, and that's
the last time we see him.

548
00:29:24,965 --> 00:29:27,092
What do you mean?
How can that be?

549
00:29:27,217 --> 00:29:28,927
I don't know.
He never gets back on the elevator

550
00:29:29,052 --> 00:29:30,845
and he's never seen
leaving the building.

551
00:29:31,012 --> 00:29:32,764
Okay, but how? Why?

552
00:29:32,889 --> 00:29:35,600
How does he leave the building
without being seen?

553
00:29:35,976 --> 00:29:37,978
If he waited until the hallway
was empty

554
00:29:38,103 --> 00:29:40,480
he could've taken the stairs
down to the first floor.

555
00:29:40,605 --> 00:29:43,358
Yeah, but how does he avoid
being seen after that?

556
00:29:43,483 --> 00:29:46,236
It...it's not like he's a guy
who doesn't stand out.

557
00:29:46,361 --> 00:29:47,487
True.

558
00:29:50,907 --> 00:29:52,242
This is the room
the murder took place.

559
00:29:52,367 --> 00:29:55,036
That's the balcony.
Doorway out here to the corridor.

560
00:29:55,161 --> 00:29:58,164
Past the stairs,
down to the elevators.

561
00:29:58,331 --> 00:30:03,670
This, if I recall, was a window
out to a fire escape.

562
00:30:03,795 --> 00:30:05,213
Could he have gone down
a fire escape?

563
00:30:05,380 --> 00:30:07,549
He's a big man.
I don't think he's that agile.

564
00:30:07,674 --> 00:30:08,717
Then what?

565
00:30:09,592 --> 00:30:11,261
Yes, it's a puzzle.

566
00:30:13,096 --> 00:30:14,764
Why do you look so happy?

567
00:30:14,889 --> 00:30:16,391
You have a way to solve
the puzzle?

568
00:30:17,851 --> 00:30:18,977
Naturally.

569
00:30:22,188 --> 00:30:23,440
Meet me at the theater.

570
00:30:30,363 --> 00:30:31,865
This is good work.

571
00:30:32,615 --> 00:30:34,326
Keep me informed
of your progress.

572
00:30:45,462 --> 00:30:48,048
Why are you lurking
out there, Mr. Jane?

573
00:30:53,011 --> 00:30:54,512
I need a favor.

574
00:30:55,305 --> 00:30:57,974
Is this the type of favor where...

575
00:30:58,641 --> 00:31:00,435
You would then owe me
a favor?

576
00:31:00,560 --> 00:31:02,479
Actually, you already
owe me a favor

577
00:31:02,604 --> 00:31:04,981
but I'll spot you another
if that pleases you.

578
00:31:08,068 --> 00:31:09,486
What do you need?

579
00:31:10,236 --> 00:31:11,821
I need you.

580
00:31:12,614 --> 00:31:15,533
Okay, 15 minutes to curtain, people.
Let's pick it up, pick it up.

581
00:31:17,911 --> 00:31:18,953
Hey.

582
00:31:20,955 --> 00:31:25,293
Deandra, don't forget to wait
three bars in the first intro, okay?

583
00:31:25,460 --> 00:31:26,544
Darling, I've been doing this

584
00:31:26,669 --> 00:31:28,630
since before you learned
to wipe your own nose.

585
00:31:29,339 --> 00:31:30,882
Worry about your own business.

586
00:31:33,551 --> 00:31:35,637
Never direct. Never direct.

587
00:31:37,806 --> 00:31:40,308
Ladies and gentlemen,
if you will kindly enter the theater

588
00:31:40,433 --> 00:31:41,851
and take your seats, please.

589
00:31:49,317 --> 00:31:51,444
Hey, Lisbon,
you at the theater yet?

590
00:31:51,569 --> 00:31:52,904
Jane, where are you?

591
00:31:53,613 --> 00:31:55,323
On my way.
Almost there.

592
00:31:55,448 --> 00:31:57,450
You head backstage.
We'll see you in a minute.

593
00:31:57,617 --> 00:31:58,993
We? Who's we?

594
00:31:59,285 --> 00:32:01,246
I'll see you very soon, Lisbon.
Bye.

595
00:32:02,664 --> 00:32:04,082
I don't know about this.

596
00:32:04,958 --> 00:32:06,543
Oh, trust me.
You'll have fun.

597
00:32:06,835 --> 00:32:09,337
"Trust me.
You'll have fun."

598
00:32:09,921 --> 00:32:12,966
Was there ever
a more suspicious phase?

599
00:32:13,091 --> 00:32:15,969
J.J., you have to live a little.

600
00:32:16,094 --> 00:32:18,721
You spend your days
chasing paper clip thieves.

601
00:32:18,888 --> 00:32:21,433
When do you ever get the chance
to nail a killer?

602
00:32:22,100 --> 00:32:24,811
No, it's true.
I'm happy to help.

603
00:32:26,563 --> 00:32:31,234
It's the performance aspect
that concerns me.

604
00:32:33,820 --> 00:32:35,280
What performance aspect?

605
00:32:35,405 --> 00:32:38,450
You go into a room.
If someone says anything to you

606
00:32:38,616 --> 00:32:41,453
you say, "Ha ha ha",
or perhaps...

607
00:32:43,496 --> 00:32:44,789
Ha ha ha.

608
00:32:46,040 --> 00:32:47,208
Perhaps.

609
00:32:49,252 --> 00:32:50,462
Good enough.

610
00:32:54,757 --> 00:32:56,134
Would an accent help?

611
00:32:58,261 --> 00:32:59,345
Keep it simple.

612
00:33:00,889 --> 00:33:02,640
As you know, this, show

613
00:33:02,807 --> 00:33:07,103
has faced a great deal of hardship
and tragedy the past few days.

614
00:33:07,604 --> 00:33:11,107
But I can tell you
that it has only made us stronger.

615
00:33:15,904 --> 00:33:17,280
Evening, Lisbon, you having fun yet?

616
00:33:17,405 --> 00:33:19,199
There you are. Where have you been
and what's your plan?

617
00:33:19,324 --> 00:33:20,992
I will tell you in one minute.

618
00:33:21,117 --> 00:33:23,369
Oh, good evening,
and what an evening it is.

619
00:33:23,536 --> 00:33:25,914
- Lisbon, you remember Mr. Dodge?
- Of course.

620
00:33:26,289 --> 00:33:28,666
Agents, what are you doing here?

621
00:33:28,833 --> 00:33:31,503
Ah, I've gotta tell you, Mr. Dodge
after watching all those rehearsals

622
00:33:31,669 --> 00:33:33,505
we couldn't just stay away,
could we?

623
00:33:33,630 --> 00:33:35,340
Okay, Warren, you're supposed
to go say something.

624
00:33:35,507 --> 00:33:36,549
Yes, I know. I'm ready.

625
00:33:36,674 --> 00:33:40,053
Finally, let me introduce
our esteemed producer

626
00:33:40,303 --> 00:33:42,138
Warren Dodge, to tell you...

627
00:33:42,263 --> 00:33:43,431
Who is that?

628
00:33:45,725 --> 00:33:47,602
Oh, my goodness.
It's Berndt Hanover.

629
00:33:48,061 --> 00:33:50,146
Mr. Hanover. Hi.

630
00:33:50,355 --> 00:33:51,981
What a wonderful surprise.

631
00:33:58,444 --> 00:33:59,904
Mr. Hanover, such an honor.

632
00:34:00,029 --> 00:34:02,156
I'm...I'm Sylvia Clare.
We spoke on the phone.

633
00:34:02,281 --> 00:34:03,699
Your trip was a good one, I hope?

634
00:34:03,824 --> 00:34:06,202
Yes, it was very good.
Thank you, Ms. Clare.

635
00:34:06,327 --> 00:34:08,371
- Sure.
- Excuse me. What's going on?

636
00:34:10,289 --> 00:34:13,376
Warren? Why don't you come out
and talk to the lovely people?

637
00:34:14,085 --> 00:34:17,672
Ms. Clare, I have come to address
my fellow investors.

638
00:34:17,797 --> 00:34:20,174
Why don't I go and speak
to them now?

639
00:34:20,299 --> 00:34:21,342
What?

640
00:34:21,467 --> 00:34:23,052
Oh, terrific.
That is a great idea.

641
00:34:23,177 --> 00:34:25,012
- Isn't it, Warren?
- Who are you, huh?

642
00:34:25,137 --> 00:34:26,264
Wait. What are you doing?

643
00:34:26,389 --> 00:34:28,224
He's not Berndt Hanover.
He's a fraud!

644
00:34:28,349 --> 00:34:30,685
Warren! What is going on?

645
00:34:30,810 --> 00:34:32,687
I don't know what game he's playing
but he's not Hanover.

646
00:34:32,812 --> 00:34:34,313
Well, what are you saying?
Then who is he?

647
00:34:34,438 --> 00:34:36,065
His name is J.J. LaRoche.

648
00:34:36,524 --> 00:34:39,569
Mr. Dodge is correct.
It isn't Berndt Hanover.

649
00:34:39,902 --> 00:34:41,737
Okay, I'm totally confused.

650
00:34:41,862 --> 00:34:45,032
Ah, we faked it...the hat, the glasses
the cane, the coat.

651
00:34:45,157 --> 00:34:48,369
- Et voila. Berndt Hanover.
- But why?

652
00:34:48,578 --> 00:34:52,790
A test. You all believed it was him
and it was easy to make you believe it.

653
00:34:53,708 --> 00:34:57,086
For about ten seconds,
until he said that I wasn't.

654
00:34:57,211 --> 00:35:01,591
And then nobody believed it anymore
just like I don't believe anymore

655
00:35:01,757 --> 00:35:03,926
that Berndt Hanover exists.

656
00:35:04,051 --> 00:35:06,220
- What? Well, that's ridiculous.
- Is it?

657
00:35:06,345 --> 00:35:07,805
Of course Berndt Hanover exists.

658
00:35:07,930 --> 00:35:10,641
Then produce him.
You're a producer. Where is he?

659
00:35:10,766 --> 00:35:14,061
Well... I just can't magically
make him appear.

660
00:35:14,186 --> 00:35:15,438
Call him.

661
00:35:15,980 --> 00:35:19,233
- Mr. Jane, I don't have his number on me.
- Hard to believe.

662
00:35:19,567 --> 00:35:21,319
You know, it's at my office

663
00:35:21,444 --> 00:35:23,237
and I will call him there,
when I'm...

664
00:35:23,362 --> 00:35:25,156
- I have it. I'll call him.
- That's lucky.

665
00:35:25,364 --> 00:35:27,325
- I'm calling him.
- Good idea.

666
00:35:28,826 --> 00:35:30,620
- It's ringing.
- Excellent.

667
00:35:31,454 --> 00:35:34,123
He might not answer.
He's a...he's a very busy man.

668
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:35,916
Of course.

669
00:35:40,254 --> 00:35:41,547
Excuse me.

670
00:35:42,757 --> 00:35:45,426
Your coat is playing
the "Fifth Symphony."

671
00:35:47,887 --> 00:35:49,555
Gonna answer that,
Mr. Dodge?

672
00:35:51,515 --> 00:35:53,517
Or should we call you mister...

673
00:35:53,976 --> 00:35:55,394
Hanover?

674
00:35:58,022 --> 00:35:59,940
Hello? Sorry. Mr. Dodge

675
00:36:00,066 --> 00:36:01,859
can't come to the phone
right now, Sylvia.

676
00:36:01,984 --> 00:36:03,903
He's a little busy getting arrested.

677
00:36:05,237 --> 00:36:07,365
Hanover didn't come down
in the elevator

678
00:36:07,490 --> 00:36:10,117
because Hanover
never even existed.

679
00:36:10,826 --> 00:36:13,913
Dodge was playing the part
from the beginning.

680
00:36:14,038 --> 00:36:16,582
He took off his disguise,
eluded the cameras

681
00:36:16,707 --> 00:36:18,209
by coming down a fire escape.

682
00:36:18,334 --> 00:36:20,336
Lisbon, he's all yours.

683
00:36:21,629 --> 00:36:23,047
You're under arrest.

684
00:36:26,634 --> 00:36:28,219
Tell me that wasn't fun.

685
00:36:29,387 --> 00:36:33,557
I cannot tell you that,
but we are even, yes?

686
00:36:33,683 --> 00:36:34,934
Yes.

687
00:36:38,688 --> 00:36:42,650
I wanted the show to work.
I was willing to do anything.

688
00:36:42,775 --> 00:36:44,735
- Even kill?
- W...

689
00:36:45,695 --> 00:36:47,238
She surprised me.

690
00:36:49,156 --> 00:36:51,450
"When I was changing
out of the Hanover costume"

691
00:36:51,575 --> 00:36:53,202
"nobody was supposed to be
in the room at that time."

692
00:36:53,327 --> 00:36:54,537
"Nobody."

693
00:36:54,704 --> 00:36:57,748
"I told her that I put every penny
I had into the show"

694
00:36:57,873 --> 00:37:00,918
"and that Hanover was necessary
to give our investors"

695
00:37:01,043 --> 00:37:02,920
"a confidence
and peace of mind"

696
00:37:03,045 --> 00:37:05,047
"and that once I had the money
I needed"

697
00:37:05,172 --> 00:37:07,091
"I would make
Hanover disappear"

698
00:37:07,591 --> 00:37:10,094
and that everything
would work out in the end.

699
00:37:10,469 --> 00:37:12,680
But she said no.

700
00:37:14,724 --> 00:37:16,517
"She wouldn't be
any part of it..."

701
00:37:17,393 --> 00:37:19,937
"That she was going to
tell Sunderland"

702
00:37:20,855 --> 00:37:23,691
"which of course meant
that everyone would find out"

703
00:37:23,816 --> 00:37:26,736
"and... that would have ruined
everything."

704
00:37:34,618 --> 00:37:37,329
Yeah. I didn't mean
for this to happen.

705
00:37:38,622 --> 00:37:40,249
So what happened next?

706
00:37:41,208 --> 00:37:44,128
I left by the fire escape.
No one saw me.

707
00:37:44,253 --> 00:37:46,964
And then I went and got
in my car around the corner

708
00:37:47,089 --> 00:37:49,675
and I drove up and introduced
myself to the police.

709
00:37:54,930 --> 00:37:57,558
This was gonna be the hit show
that put me back on top.

710
00:37:58,476 --> 00:38:00,186
Everyone would have benefited.

711
00:38:01,812 --> 00:38:03,898
If only she hadn't
walked through that door.

712
00:38:20,372 --> 00:38:24,502
I, I didn't have
nearly enough time with her.

713
00:38:25,461 --> 00:38:28,631
And I so loved the person
that she was and respected her.

714
00:38:31,801 --> 00:38:34,094
Thank you for giving her justice.

715
00:38:34,595 --> 00:38:35,888
It means a lot.

716
00:38:37,056 --> 00:38:38,224
I'm sorry.

717
00:38:39,058 --> 00:38:41,060
Embarrassing.
I have to take this.

718
00:38:41,185 --> 00:38:42,436
- Excuse me.
- Of course.

719
00:38:42,645 --> 00:38:45,397
- Hello?
- "Patrick, it's Sheila from the hospital."

720
00:38:45,523 --> 00:38:46,982
They're bringing him
out of the coma.

721
00:38:47,733 --> 00:38:48,859
When?

722
00:38:49,735 --> 00:38:51,070
You need to get over here.

723
00:38:51,195 --> 00:38:53,697
Okay. Um... s... I...

724
00:38:54,782 --> 00:38:57,117
He's conscious,
but keep an eye on him.

725
00:38:57,243 --> 00:38:58,786
Hit that button,
and we'll be right in.

726
00:38:58,911 --> 00:39:00,120
Okay, thank you, nurse.

727
00:39:09,755 --> 00:39:12,591
You are one tough S.O.B.,
Jason Lennon.

728
00:39:12,716 --> 00:39:14,385
You really are.

729
00:39:16,095 --> 00:39:17,513
Where am I?

730
00:39:19,557 --> 00:39:21,684
What happened?

731
00:39:21,809 --> 00:39:24,061
You're in Sacramento.
You got shot.

732
00:39:24,979 --> 00:39:29,108
- Do you remember that?
- Yes, I do.

733
00:39:30,568 --> 00:39:31,944
Lorelei Martins.

734
00:39:32,069 --> 00:39:34,822
Correct. Very good.
Good. Your mind is clear.

735
00:39:34,947 --> 00:39:37,366
Now listen, I have a question
for you, Jason.

736
00:39:38,826 --> 00:39:39,994
Look at me.

737
00:39:40,452 --> 00:39:41,871
Look close.

738
00:39:42,621 --> 00:39:44,081
You recognize me?

739
00:39:44,832 --> 00:39:46,500
You ever seen me before?

740
00:39:47,668 --> 00:39:48,711
No.

741
00:39:48,961 --> 00:39:50,754
Are you sure?
It's important.

742
00:39:52,339 --> 00:39:53,591
Yes.

743
00:39:54,717 --> 00:39:56,093
I'm sure.

744
00:39:57,428 --> 00:39:59,722
That's good to know.
Thanks.

745
00:40:02,892 --> 00:40:04,184
Who are you?

746
00:40:04,310 --> 00:40:05,728
I'm a friend.

747
00:40:22,786 --> 00:40:24,997
Let's face facts, Jason.

748
00:40:25,164 --> 00:40:27,625
If you recover and live

749
00:40:28,292 --> 00:40:32,671
you'll spend life in prison for murder...
the plaything of monsters.

750
00:40:39,970 --> 00:40:43,641
I just hope that if I'm
ever in your shoes

751
00:40:44,183 --> 00:40:46,977
I have a friend close enough
to do this for me.

752
00:40:47,686 --> 00:40:49,104
Wait.

753
00:40:51,190 --> 00:40:52,775
Now don't be scared.

754
00:40:53,400 --> 00:40:54,777
This'll be quick.

755
00:40:55,736 --> 00:40:57,529
With very little pain.

756
00:41:08,040 --> 00:41:10,084
It's okay.
It's all right.

757
00:41:11,627 --> 00:41:13,837
It's okay.
I'm here for you.

758
00:41:14,171 --> 00:41:16,048
I won't leave you.

759
00:41:30,229 --> 00:41:32,147
Help! Nurse!

760
00:41:32,272 --> 00:41:33,315
Nurse!

761
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:34,483
He's in V-fib.

762
00:41:34,608 --> 00:41:36,360
- Get that crash cart over here.
- "Code blue, code blue in 116"

763
00:41:36,485 --> 00:41:38,487
"Code blue in 116"

764
00:41:39,905 --> 00:41:42,366
"Code blue, code blue in 116"

765
00:41:42,491 --> 00:41:46,662
- "Code blue in 116"
- 300. Clear.

766
00:41:46,787 --> 00:41:48,163
I'll get Mike.

767
00:41:49,289 --> 00:41:52,126
- No response.
- Grace, get Mike...

768
00:41:52,251 --> 00:41:53,335
What happened?

769
00:41:54,003 --> 00:41:55,379
Patrick, I'm sorry.

770
00:41:56,588 --> 00:41:58,173
His heart gave out.

771
00:42:00,634 --> 00:42:02,386
Never said a word.

